WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.836 --> 00:00:04.838
This is a hiking trail.

00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:07.841
When you look at it, or any other trail
for that matter

00:00:07.841 --> 00:00:10.043
It can feel sort of organic.

00:00:10.043 --> 00:00:13.380
Like nobody in particular decided 
what it should look like.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:14.881
But behind almost every trail

00:00:14.881 --> 00:00:18.518
are planners, builders, and designers 
crafting an experience

00:00:18.518 --> 00:00:20.487
that gives people a tiny taste

00:00:20.487 --> 00:00:23.289
of what real wilderness feels like.

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:25.225
And when they do their job right...

00:00:25.225 --> 00:00:27.460
it can feel like they were never there.

00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:35.702
It begins with trying to understand

00:00:35.702 --> 00:00:38.271
the visitors who will be using the trails.

00:00:39.072 --> 00:00:41.074
Jeff Marian studies the design of trails

00:00:41.074 --> 00:00:42.742
and how they fit into the environment.

00:00:42.742 --> 00:00:45.178
People seem to really want to hike on

00:00:45.178 --> 00:00:48.615
native surface trails, you know, 
in pristine nature

00:00:48.615 --> 00:00:52.652
not highly altered, highly developed  
nature with pavements

00:00:52.652 --> 00:00:53.887
and things like that.

00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:57.323
So what makes a trail feel 
like pristine nature?

00:00:57.323 --> 00:00:59.225
For one thing, there's its shape.

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:01.928
Unlike paved roads, trails are 
often built to mimic

00:01:01.928 --> 00:01:06.666
the imperfect natural patterns of 
branches, waterways, or lightning.

00:01:06.666 --> 00:01:09.936
There's also the features a trail 
connects with along the way.

00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:13.339
Designers will often build a trail
so it touches anchors —

00:01:13.339 --> 00:01:16.009
vertical features like large rocks or trees

00:01:16.009 --> 00:01:18.611
that create a pleasant frame on 
the edges of a trail.

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:21.681
They also have to decide where it goes.

00:01:22.348 --> 00:01:23.683
So you sit down on your map

00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:25.718
and you map out these control points.

00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:28.822
Designers will identify the places
people shouldn't go.

00:01:28.822 --> 00:01:31.057
Like hazardous zones, private property

00:01:31.057 --> 00:01:33.293
and environmentally sensitive areas.

00:01:33.293 --> 00:01:34.661
And the places they should.

00:01:34.661 --> 00:01:37.730
Like scenic overlooks,
waterfalls and lakes.

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:40.667
These are called negative
and positive control points

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:44.370
and they give a rough idea of 
where a trail can go

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.306
making sure a trail goes to the places

00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:49.175
where hikers will want to go is essential

00:01:49.175 --> 00:01:50.710
because if a trail doesn't

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:53.012
curious hikers will find 
their own way to them.

00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:56.516
Creating desire lines that branch off 
from the actual trail.

00:01:56.516 --> 00:01:59.552
Those desire lines can disrupt
water systems, plants

00:01:59.552 --> 00:02:03.389
or wildlife in ways the trail designers 
didn't account for.

00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:05.291
And that's the paradox of trail design.

00:02:05.291 --> 00:02:06.926
They're artificial creations

00:02:06.926 --> 00:02:09.362
that need to feel like
they're part of nature

00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:11.564
but also need to accommodate visitors

00:02:11.564 --> 00:02:14.200
all without deteriorating over time.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:17.103
Designers spend a lot of time 
thinking about this.

00:02:17.103 --> 00:02:20.140
But the force they're most concerned
about protecting trails from

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:22.342
actually isn't people.

00:02:22.342 --> 00:02:23.910
It's water.

00:02:24.711 --> 00:02:28.148
Water is the natural enemy 
of a manmade trail.

00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:29.816
It can erode a trail away,

00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:31.351
make it too muddy to use,

00:02:31.351 --> 00:02:33.653
or change the shape of it completely.

00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:35.255
So we try to design trails

00:02:35.255 --> 00:02:38.491
to be sort of invisible to that water flow

00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:40.693
and that movement of water
across the landscape.

00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:42.795
So hydrologically invisible.

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:45.031
To do that, look at topographic maps

00:02:45.031 --> 00:02:47.500
which illustrate the landscapes elevation.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:48.935
And they look at the fall line.

00:02:48.935 --> 00:02:52.138
That's the line that water follows
as it flows downhill.

00:02:52.138 --> 00:02:55.141
If you dumped a bucket of water 
on the ground on a slope

00:02:55.141 --> 00:02:57.677
it would run straight down 
the fall line.

00:02:57.677 --> 00:03:01.548
If a trail runs parallel to the fall line 
or too close to it

00:03:01.548 --> 00:03:04.050
it becomes a pathway for water
when it rains.

00:03:04.417 --> 00:03:07.754
The trail then sort of acts 
as a drainage ditch

00:03:07.754 --> 00:03:09.622
going straight down the hill.

00:03:09.622 --> 00:03:13.193
Over time, that causes the trail 
to erode and become wider.

00:03:13.193 --> 00:03:16.462
But a trail that runs perpendicular 
to the fall line isn't good either

00:03:16.462 --> 00:03:18.932
because then water can't flow 
downhill at all.

00:03:18.932 --> 00:03:20.934
If it's dead level, it's going 
to sit there

00:03:20.934 --> 00:03:22.635
and make a big, huge mudhole.

00:03:22.635 --> 00:03:25.104
It's mostly just, you know, poor design.

00:03:25.538 --> 00:03:28.308
Instead, a good trail runs diagonal 
to the fall line

00:03:28.308 --> 00:03:31.044
cutting up a slope at an angle
and allowing water

00:03:31.044 --> 00:03:33.880
to flow across the trail
instead of down it.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.482
And accounting for the fall
line is just one of the principles

00:03:36.482 --> 00:03:39.485
designers look at to manage 
water on trails.

00:03:39.485 --> 00:03:42.322
There's also grade reversals
where a trail alternates

00:03:42.322 --> 00:03:44.991
between short, uphill 
and downhill sections.

00:03:44.991 --> 00:03:46.426
This creates little valleys

00:03:46.426 --> 00:03:48.361
where water can flow off of a trail

00:03:48.361 --> 00:03:50.063
in smaller quantities.

00:03:50.063 --> 00:03:51.064
There's the slope

00:03:51.064 --> 00:03:54.133
which, when it stays between 
a 3% and 10% grade

00:03:54.133 --> 00:03:57.437
is both comfortable for hiking 
and minimizes erosion.

00:03:57.437 --> 00:04:00.340
There's the out slope,
a slight downhill tilt on the trail

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:01.941
that helps water flow downhill.

00:04:01.941 --> 00:04:04.677
And there's the tread: the stuff
the trail is actually made of.

00:04:04.677 --> 00:04:08.448
The higher the proportion of rocks, 
the better it can resist erosion.

00:04:08.448 --> 00:04:12.218
This careful combination of 
alignment, materials, and shape

00:04:12.218 --> 00:04:14.988
can help a trail last 
through years of impact...

00:04:16.623 --> 00:04:20.193
even as those impacts become bigger
than they've ever been before.

00:04:20.193 --> 00:04:22.662
We're getting bigger rain events

00:04:22.662 --> 00:04:25.231
and they're having a real toll on trails.

00:04:25.231 --> 00:04:30.470
And so trail sustainability needs to focus 
way more on tread drainage these days

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:33.172
than we ever used to.

00:04:33.172 --> 00:04:35.241
A lot of the work that trail designers do

00:04:35.241 --> 00:04:37.710
isn't actually coming up with new trails.

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:40.280
It's strengthening and updating old ones

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:41.547
many of which have been around

00:04:41.547 --> 00:04:44.884
since before recreational hiking was 
even something people did.

00:04:44.884 --> 00:04:45.918
It's challenging.

00:04:45.918 --> 00:04:48.721
It's not rocket science,
but it is challenging.

00:04:48.721 --> 00:04:51.557
It requires kind of an 
ecological knowledge

00:04:51.557 --> 00:04:53.226
of the inner relationship

00:04:53.226 --> 00:04:55.695
of a wide number of different factors.

00:04:56.129 --> 00:04:59.666
Out on a trail, these factors 
add up to an experience...

00:05:00.366 --> 00:05:01.401
a feeling...

00:05:01.401 --> 00:05:03.369
of seeing a world that we didn't make.

00:05:04.404 --> 00:05:07.640
It's a world we don't often 
have a way to see

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:10.209
and a good trail is our ticket in.

00:05:17.784 --> 00:05:35.234
finding.

